Compost and nutrient cycling (@farming-compost: 0, is #trail, #contains @farming-compost-inputs, #contains @farming-compost-batch, #contains @farming-compost-troubleshooting, #contains @farming-compost-maturity, #contains @eisenia-fetida, #contains @farming-manure, #contains @farming-compost-contaminants) { r_65Y9FF8CM0H3HY7V9DMFXFF22Z
Composting is controlled biological decomposition. It returns organic matter and some nutrients; it does not create minerals or replace all crop fertilization. Learn a repeatable batch before experimenting with additives.
} r_65Y9FF8CM0H3HY7V9DMFXFF22Z

Choose clean ingredients (@farming-compost-inputs: 0, #part-of @farming-compost, #source @farm-source-epa-compost, #source @farm-source-maryland-compost) { r_2C50HV9DV4BQF5JHDX7844TX5Z
Browns supply much carbon: dry leaves, straw, shredded uncoated cardboard, and woody trimmings. Greens supply more nitrogen: fresh weeds without seed, food scraps, grass, and coffee grounds. Colour is not the classification: brown coffee grounds still count as greens.

Start with two to three loose volumes of browns per volume of greens; adjust by moisture, smell, and heating. This volume recipe is not a measured carbon:nitrogen ratio. Microbes commonly work well near 30:1 C:N by mass.

Chop coarse material, mix rather than making thick grass layers, and retain some structure for air. Exclude plastics, coated paper, treated timber, pet or human faeces, oils, meat, and dairy from an ordinary backyard pile. Keep persistent weeds, seed heads, and diseased plants out unless a validated process handles them.

Practice: inventory a week's residues and find a reliable dry-leaf supply. [EPA method](https://www.epa.gov/recycle/composting-home); [materials and C:N](https://extension.umd.edu/resource/how-make-compost-home).
} r_2C50HV9DV4BQF5JHDX7844TX5Z

Build and monitor a batch (@farming-compost-batch: 0, #part-of @farming-compost, #source @farm-source-ncsu-compost) { r_2T3MJAS0KANRVVCVXX8F5FSDP3
Use an aerated, animal-resistant bay on a site where runoff cannot reach water or harvest handling. A mixed pile around 1 m³ retains heat better than a thin layer. Protect against heavy rain without sealing out air.

Moisten ingredients throughout: a squeezed handful should feel like a wrung sponge, not drip freely. Mix browns and greens. Keep the active batch separate from new scraps, curing compost, and finished compost; three bays or marked containers make this easy.

Measure temperature in several places and record it daily during active heating. Roughly 55–65°C is a useful hot-compost operating range; turn and aerate if overheating or oxygen loss develops. Rebuild with outside material moved inward. A hot centre alone does not demonstrate that every particle was sanitized.

Practice: record ingredients, approximate volume, moisture, temperatures, turns, and smell until the batch stops reheating. [Composting methods](https://content.ces.ncsu.edu/extension-gardener-handbook/2-composting).
} r_2T3MJAS0KANRVVCVXX8F5FSDP3

Fix the cause, not the smell (@farming-compost-troubleshooting: 0, #part-of @farming-compost, #source @farm-source-minnesota-compost) { r_5HS3DY7HDG28WCVHRB3RD1DNVS
| Observation | Likely cause | Action |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sour smell, sludge | Too wet; little air | Mix in dry coarse browns, turn, protect from rain |
| Ammonia smell | Excess readily available nitrogen | Add browns and mix; stop adding manure or grass |
| Dry, unchanged material | Too little moisture | Moisten while turning |
| Moist but no heating | Small pile, little nitrogen, or already decomposed | Check stage; combine material or add greens if still raw |
| Dense grass mats | Poor structure | Break apart and mix with dry leaves |
| Flies or rodents | Exposed food or unsuitable inputs | Bury scraps inside mixed material; secure the enclosure |

Cold compost still decomposes, usually more slowly, but should not be credited with killing seeds or pathogens. Buying microbial starters is usually unnecessary; suitable feedstocks already carry decomposers.

Practice: change one condition, then observe temperature and smell over several days. [Home-garden composting](https://extension.umn.edu/managing-soil-and-nutrients/composting-home-gardens).
} r_5HS3DY7HDG28WCVHRB3RD1DNVS

Cure, check, and apply (@farming-compost-maturity: 0, #part-of @farming-compost, #source @farm-source-compost-use) { r_4VJFST6EERWZ2BBQKHC5FEW4S0
After active decomposition, let the batch cure. Finished compost is crumbly, smells earthy, and does not heat substantially after turning and moistening. Large woody fragments may remain; screen and return them to the next batch.

Compare germination and growth in a modest compost/medium mixture against clean medium. Poor performance can indicate immaturity, salts, or contamination; a home test cannot certify pathogen or chemical safety. Test nutrient content and salts when applying substantial or repeated quantities.

Apply according to soil and compost tests, crop needs, and existing phosphorus. Surface-dress established beds or incorporate shallowly during preparation. Do not make repeated thick manure-compost additions by habit. Keep compost away from trunks and avoid pure compost as a universal seed-starting medium.

Practice: compare a measured small application with an untreated strip, keeping the crop and irrigation alike. [Making and using compost](https://extension.missouri.edu/publications/g6956).
} r_4VJFST6EERWZ2BBQKHC5FEW4S0

Red compost worm — Eisenia fetida (@eisenia-fetida: 0, is #species, #part-of @farming-compost, #source @farm-source-worms) { r_33JMAHVAMGMAAX69CCHYDGF9VQ
Lifecycle: cocoon, juvenile, mature worm, new cocoons. Use compost worms in a shallow ventilated bin; ordinary deep-soil earthworms are not interchangeable.

Start with moist shredded cardboard or leaf bedding. Keep shaded, draining, and roughly 15–25°C; protect from heat, freezing, ants, and flooding. Add small amounts of chopped plant scraps under bedding. Feed again only as material disappears. Avoid overfeeding, salt, oils, meat, and dairy; never put worms in an actively hot pile.

Harvest when most food is converted by shifting feed to one side and separating finished material. Return worms and cocoons to fresh bedding. Reproduction and processing rate depend on temperature, stocking, and feed; weigh inputs rather than promising a fixed daily rate.

Liquid draining from the bin is leachate, not finished compost tea. Keep it off edible leaves and out of watercourses. [Vermiculture reference](https://content.ces.ncsu.edu/raising-earthworms-successfully).
} r_33JMAHVAMGMAAX69CCHYDGF9VQ

Manure belongs in a managed nutrient system (@farming-manure: 0, #part-of @farming-compost, #source @farm-source-soil-management, #source @farm-source-manure-safety) { r_2SVP8RM1FGVT1Q75VFNMP9VRKK
Collect manure with dry bedding, prevent runoff, and match additions to soil nutrient budgets. Fresh manure can carry pathogens, weed seed, salts, and residues. Age, dark colour, and an earthy smell do not establish sanitation; worm processing is not a validated kill step.

For vegetables, prefer compost made with a documented sanitation process. Where raw or inadequately processed manure is used, follow local food-safety requirements and keep it away from growing edible surfaces. As a named reference, US organic production uses at least 120 days from incorporation to harvest for soil-contact edible portions, or 90 days for those without soil contact. These are not universal legal rules or a guarantee of safety.

Keep manure out of sprout production and harvest washing water. Animal areas, compost tools, and clean produce tools need separate handling.

Practice: draw the manure-to-compost-to-field route and identify every runoff or cross-contamination point. [Manure management](https://extension.psu.edu/managing-soils); [food-safety guidance](https://www.ams.usda.gov/grades-standards/soil-building-manures-composts).
} r_2SVP8RM1FGVT1Q75VFNMP9VRKK

Persistent residues and common shortcuts (@farming-compost-contaminants: 0, #part-of @farming-compost, #source @farm-source-herbicide) { r_76VJQD4RZGN4AHDEMRVG4WY6C1
Ask where hay, straw, grass, and manure came from. Some pasture herbicides survive animal digestion and ordinary composting and can injure beans and tomatoes. Before using an unfamiliar batch widely, compare sensitive plants grown with a small sample against an uncontaminated control; consult local extension if distorted growth appears.

Do not add lime or ash by habit: they can raise pH, add salts, and increase ammonia loss. Eggshells decompose slowly and do not quickly cure a calcium problem. Banana peels are feedstock, not a balanced fertilizer. Coffee grounds belong mixed with other material, not in a thick surface mat.

Bokashi ferments scraps; the material still needs further decomposition or managed soil incorporation before planting. Drying scraps in an appliance also does not produce mature compost.

Practice: compare seedling growth in control medium and medium containing the proposed amendment. [Herbicide carryover and bioassays](https://content.ces.ncsu.edu/herbicide-carryover).
} r_76VJQD4RZGN4AHDEMRVG4WY6C1
